  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Another breezy day is forecast as northeast winds \ncarry fast-moving showers onshore from time to time. Last evening's \n00z XMR sounding revealed sufficient low-level moisture below 750-\n800 mb, which will continue to support shower activity today (rain \nchances 20-35%). Despite a slightly weaker pressure gradient today, \nlow pressure off of the Carolina Coast and high pressure situated to \nour north will support sustained winds 15-20 mph (20-25 mph at the \ncoast) and peak gusts of 25-35 mph. Based on REFS/HREF probabilities \nless than 10-20% for frequent 35+ mph gusts, we look to fall just \nshort of Wind Advisory criteria. That said, occasionally higher \ngusts are expected, especially at the coast. \n\nRough, large breaking waves of 6-10 feet and life-threatening rip \ncurrents will make for a dangerous surf zone. Stay out of the water \ntoday! A High Surf Advisory and high risk for rip currents continues \nat all central Florida Atlantic beaches. Long period swells will \nalso lead to minor/moderate beach erosion following high tide \ncycles. \n\nHigh temperatures may warm by a degree or two compared to yesterday \nbut generally range from the upper 70s north to the low and middle \n80s Orlando/Cape Canaveral southward.\n\nTuesday-Wednesday...Weak mid level troughing will remain over \nFlorida through mid week. High pressure centered well north of us is \ngoing to support north-northeast flow with gusty daytime winds \n(especially along the coast). Peak gusts reaching 20-30 mph are \nanticipated, especially on Wednesday across coastal Volusia County. \nThe low pressure system over the western Atlantic slowly pulls \nnorthward, allowing some drier air to push south across the FL \nPeninsula Tuesday. The surface-to-700mb layer moistens again on \nWednesday, so some isolated marine showers are forecast by Wednesday \nafternoon/evening. For now, mention of rain making it to the coast \nis confined to areas from Sebastian southward. Adjustments to rain \nchances are possible, especially for coastal locations, as medium-\nrange models tend to struggle with these shallow-layer precip \npatterns. \n\nA High Surf Advisory and high risk for life-threatening rip currents \nwill continue Tuesday, and the high risk for rip currents persists \nfurther into Wednesday as well. Entering the water is strongly \ndiscouraged! \n\nThursday-Sunday...Upper level ridging builds across the central U.S. \nlate week, extending from the Rio Grande all the way north to the \nGreat Lakes. Eventually, this upper high gets squeezed and weakened \nby a developing longwave trough over the Upper Plains Saturday into \nSunday. Two surface features to watch from Friday into the weekend \nare 1) an expanding high across the eastern/northeastern U.S. and 2) \ndeveloping low pressure along a remnant front that stretches across \nthe Florida Straits and Bahamas. As currently presented, the \npressure gradient strengthens and makes for a windy/gusty weekend \nahead. Rough surf, dangerous rip currents, and the risk for \nadditional beach erosion would also result. Rain chances grow Friday \nnight onward as moisture pools along the aforementioned front, \nespecially from the Cape southward.\n\nDaytime temperatures will change little from days 4 through 7 (upper \n70s to low 80s), with slightly warmer overnight lows due to \nstrengthened onshore flow Friday onward. Overall, values look to run \na few degrees cooler than mid-October normals.",
